{
  "id": "c7",
  "title": "Writing Functions",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "c7-l1",
      "title": "Your Own Function",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "main is a function, and so are printf and scanf. You can write your own: a name for a piece of code that does one job, used as often as you like.",
        "int square(int x) means: square takes an int called x, and gives back an int. return sends the result back to whoever called the function.",
        "Unix was built from thousands of small functions. Kernighan and Ritchie's advice: if a piece of code is used twice, give it a name."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int square(int x) {",
          "    return x * x;",
          "}",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    printf(\"%d\\n\", square(7));",
          "    printf(\"%d\\n\", square(3) + square(4));",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "49",
          "25"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c7-l1-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "In int square(int x), what does the first int mean?",
          "options": [
            "square gives back an int",
            "x is the first value",
            "square runs once",
            "The function is called int"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The type before the name is the type of the result."
        },
        {
          "id": "c7-l1-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does return do?",
          "options": [
            "Ends the function and hands back a value",
            "Prints the value",
            "Starts the function again",
            "Returns to main's first line"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The value after return becomes the value of the call, like square(7)."
        },
        {
          "id": "c7-l1-e3",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this program print?",
          "code": [
            "#include <stdio.h>",
            "",
            "int twice(int n) {",
            "    return n + n;",
            "}",
            "",
            "int main() {",
            "    printf(\"%d\\n\", twice(5));",
            "    return 0;",
            "}"
          ],
          "options": [
            "10",
            "5",
            "25",
            "7"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "twice(5) returns 5 + 5."
        },
        {
          "id": "c7-l1-e4",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Return the bigger number.",
          "code": [
            "int max(int a, int b) {",
            "    if (a > b)",
            "        ___ a;",
            "    return b;",
            "}"
          ],
          "options": [
            "return",
            "print",
            "break",
            "give"
          ],
          "answer": "return",
          "explanation": "return a; hands a back and ends the function."
        },
        {
          "id": "c7-l1-e5",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This program doesn't compile. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "#include <stdio.h>",
            "",
            "int cube(int x) {",
            "    return x * x * x;",
            "}",
            "",
            "int main() {",
            "    printf(\"%d\\n\", cube());",
            "    return 0;",
            "}"
          ],
          "answer": 7,
          "explanation": "cube needs one argument, like cube(3)."
        },
        {
          "id": "c7-l1-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why write functions?",
          "options": [
            "Each job gets a name, is written once and can be used many times",
            "Programs run faster without main",
            "C requires one per line",
            "To save paper"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Small named pieces make big programs readable and easy to fix."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c7-l2",
      "title": "void Functions",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Not every function computes a value. Some just do something, like print a line. Their result type is void, which means nothing. They don't need a return value.",
        "A function with no arguments has empty brackets, or (void). You still need the brackets when you call it: line();",
        "A function can call other functions, and the program jumps back to the right place every time."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "void line(void) {",
          "    printf(\"----------\\n\");",
          "}",
          "",
          "void title(void) {",
          "    line();",
          "    printf(\"  REPORT\\n\");",
          "    line();",
          "}",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    title();",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "----------",
          "  REPORT",
          "----------"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c7-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does void mean as a result type?",
          "options": [
            "The function gives back nothing",
            "The function is empty",
            "It returns 0",
            "It can't be called"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "void functions do a job instead of computing a value."
        },
        {
          "id": "c7-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How do you call void beep(void)?",
          "options": [
            "beep();",
            "beep;",
            "call beep;",
            "void beep();"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The brackets are needed, even when they're empty."
        },
        {
          "id": "c7-l2-e3",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How many dashed lines does the example print?",
          "options": [
            "2",
            "1",
            "3",
            "0"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "title calls line twice."
        },
        {
          "id": "c7-l2-e4",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Write a function that gives back nothing.",
          "code": [
            "___ hello(void) {",
            "    printf(\"hello\\n\");",
            "}"
          ],
          "options": [
            "void",
            "int",
            "return",
            "none"
          ],
          "answer": "void",
          "explanation": "void as the result type."
        },
        {
          "id": "c7-l2-e5",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This program doesn't compile. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "#include <stdio.h>",
            "",
            "void greet(void) {",
            "    printf(\"hi\\n\");",
            "}",
            "",
            "int main() {",
            "    int x = greet();",
            "    return 0;",
            "}"
          ],
          "answer": 7,
          "explanation": "greet is void: it gives back nothing, so there's nothing to store in x."
        },
        {
          "id": "c7-l2-e6",
          "type": "order_lines",
          "prompt": "Put the lines in order: a function stars() that prints *** and is called from main.",
          "lines": [
            "#include <stdio.h>",
            "void stars(void) {",
            "    printf(\"***\\n\");",
            "}",
            "int main() {",
            "    stars();",
            "    return 0;",
            "}"
          ],
          "answer": [
            "#include <stdio.h>",
            "void stars(void) {",
            "    printf(\"***\\n\");",
            "}",
            "int main() {",
            "    stars();",
            "    return 0;",
            "}"
          ],
          "explanation": "The function comes first, then main, which calls it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c7-l3",
      "title": "Declare Before Use",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "The C compiler reads the file from top to bottom. To call a function that's written further down, tell the compiler about it first with a prototype: int square(int x); with a semicolon and no body.",
        "Prototypes are what header files like stdio.h are full of: printf's prototype is in there, the code for it is in the C library.",
        "A common style puts the prototypes at the top, main next, and the functions after it, so the file reads like a table of contents."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "double average(int a, int b);",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    printf(\"%.1f\\n\", average(4, 7));",
          "    return 0;",
          "}",
          "",
          "double average(int a, int b) {",
          "    return (a + b) / 2.0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "5.5"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c7-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is a prototype?",
          "options": [
            "The first line of a function, with a semicolon, telling the compiler it exists",
            "A copy of main",
            "An unfinished program",
            "A comment"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "It announces the name, the result type and the argument types."
        },
        {
          "id": "c7-l3-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is inside stdio.h?",
          "options": [
            "Prototypes for printf, scanf and friends",
            "The code of every program",
            "Your functions",
            "Nothing"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The compiler checks your calls against these prototypes."
        },
        {
          "id": "c7-l3-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Announce a function that's written further down.",
          "code": [
            "int add(int a, int b)___",
            "",
            "int main() {",
            "    printf(\"%d\\n\", add(2, 3));",
            "    return 0;",
            "}"
          ],
          "options": [
            ";",
            "{",
            "()",
            ":"
          ],
          "answer": ";",
          "explanation": "A prototype ends with a semicolon instead of a body."
        },
        {
          "id": "c7-l3-e4",
